# PR #1351 review comments — cdz-kernel/src/reducer.rs (v-agent-harness)

Mirrored from https://github.com/camshaft/cadenza/pull/1351 (PR: "cand: v-agent-harness — 466d9d81b").

## 1. ⚠ Blanket `impl<R: Reducer> AsyncReducer for R` blocks the cooperative-yield migration (Copilot, reducer.rs:154) — design/coherence
> The blanket `impl<R: Reducer> AsyncReducer for R` makes it impossible for a type that already
> implements `Reducer` (e.g. `ComponentReducer`) to later provide a real async/yielding
> `AsyncReducer` implementation — the blanket impl will conflict. This contradicts the doc comment
> that such reducers can "override this" and will block the intended cooperative-yield migration.

This is the important one: a blanket impl over all `R: Reducer` means Rust coherence forbids ANY
`Reducer` type from writing its own `AsyncReducer` — so `ComponentReducer` (the whole point of the
future fuel_async_yield migration) can never provide a yielding impl, and the doc's "can override
this" is false. Consider an explicit adapter/newtype (`SyncAsAsync<R>`) instead of the blanket impl,
so real async reducers remain expressible.

## 2. `pollster_block_on` spins forever on `Poll::Pending` (Copilot, reducer.rs:241) — robustness
> `pollster_block_on` will spin forever if the future ever returns `Poll::Pending` because the waker
> is a no-op (nothing will ever wake it). Since this helper is only meant for immediately-ready
> futures, poll once and fail fast on `Pending` (and use `std::pin::pin!` to avoid the unsafe
> pinning).

Poll once; on `Pending`, fail fast (panic/error) rather than busy-spinning against a no-op waker —
and `std::pin::pin!` removes the unsafe pinning.

## 3. Test hard-codes the blanket-impl cast (Copilot, reducer.rs:213) — test/design
> This test encodes the coherence-blocking blanket impl by casting `&reducer` directly to `&dyn
> AsyncReducer`. If `AsyncReducer` needs to be overridable for `ComponentReducer` later, prefer
> exercising an explicit adapter type here so the test doesn't rely on (or force you to keep) the
> blanket impl.

Related to point 1: if you move to an adapter, this test should exercise the adapter, not the blanket
cast that locks in the coherence problem.
